WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.380
Every instance of human subject research
introduces a physical or

2
00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:09.060
psychological risk. Protecting the
participant requires a rigid grid of

3
00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:11.380
regulations to manage the process.

4
00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:15.800
This risk exists because of the tension
between the pursuit of new knowledge

5
00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:19.580
and the moral obligation to the person
providing that data.

6
00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:24.580
These risks are present in every field,
from high-stakes biomedical clinical

7
00:00:24.620 --> 00:00:28.680
trials to psychological surveys and
sociological observations.

8
00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:33.360
Historically, early human experimentation
was conducted without any

9
00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:36.340
standardized ethical protections or
oversight.

10
00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:41.000
Without systemic boundaries, the
institutional drive for data can lead to

11
00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:45.040
situations where scientific goals are
prioritized over the dignity of the

12
00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:49.940
research subject. Consequently, modern
ethical regulations are highly

13
00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:53.980
reactionary, built specifically to prevent
the recurrence of historical

14
00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:59.220
tragedies. These regulations function as a
mandatory defense system forged

15
00:00:59.260 --> 00:01:02.604
directly from past failures to safeguard
against future expositions.
